    "description": "A city-scale crowd simulation tracks approximately 20,000 pedestrians, 400 bicycles, and moving service vehicles. During each simulation tick, the CollisionBroadphase component asks which participants are within six meters of a pedestrian, the InfluenceField component finds objects inside a circular zone, and the CameraFocus component removes distant pedestrians from view. Each query currently scans every participant, causing millions of irrelevant distance calculations and pushing the tick beyond its 16 millisecond budget. The population is sparse outside dense plazas, so most comparisons involve a small neighborhood, but participants can cross map boundaries and may move substantially between ticks. The chosen organization must support frequent insertions, removals, and position changes while keeping local radius and rectangle queries practical. It must also preserve a stable participant identifier for the simulation state and avoid requiring exact global ordering of query results unless the caller requests it. The main decision is how participants should be stored and maintained so local searches examine nearby entities instead of the entire population.",
    "decision_statement": "Choose the data organization and maintenance strategy for participants that makes position-based neighborhood queries efficient despite frequent movement.",
    "notes": "Spatial-partition fits because radius and rectangle queries must visit nearby participants instead of scanning the whole population despite frequent movement; data-locality is the performance-axis sibling and update-method supports per-tick movement."
  },
